sections.body:
Policy L34220 v40 (eff 10/23/2025) states lumbar MRI may be indicated for a patient with a 'red-flag' condition such as suspected tumor, infection, herniated disc with nerve compression, or major neurological problem.
{{Q:P1}}
Red flags include history of cancer and pain that worsens when supine or at night.
{{Q:P2}}
Supportive documentation evidencing the condition and treatment is expected in the medical record.
{{Q:P3}}
The medical record must support a contemplated diagnosis or treatment change derived from MRI findings.
{{Q:P4}}
When findings will not affect treatment choices, the test is not reasonable or necessary.
{{Q:P5}}

Chart notes document:
History of cancer.
{{Q:E1}}
Pain that worsens when supine or at night.
{{Q:E2}}
Low back pain began 07/19/2026.
{{Q:E3}}
No conservative treatments initiated to date.
{{Q:E4}}
Both history of cancer and pain worsening supine/night are recorded.
{{Q:E5}}
MRI ordered to evaluate for spinal metastasis given cancer history.
{{Q:E6}}

The denial asserts imaging is not medically necessary within the first month of symptoms. Policy L34220 v40 does not specify a one-month waiting period; instead, it identifies red-flag conditions that indicate immediate MRI. The patient presents two red flags: history of cancer and pain worsening when supine or at night.
{{Q:P1}}
{{Q:P2}}
{{Q:E5}}
These red flags indicate a suspected tumor (spinal metastasis), a condition explicitly listed as a red-flag indication for lumbar MRI.
{{Q:P1}}
{{Q:E6}}
The medical record supports a contemplated diagnosis change: the MRI is ordered to rule out spinal metastasis. If metastasis is identified, treatment would shift to oncology referral, systemic therapy, or palliative radiation; if negative, alternative diagnostic workup would be pursued. This satisfies the policy requirement that the medical record support a contemplated diagnosis or treatment change derived from MRI findings.
{{Q:P4}}
Because the findings would directly affect treatment choices, the test is reasonable and necessary despite symptom duration.
{{Q:P5}}
